== ATX and table structural-whitespace audit: H15 vs C vs an INDEPENDENT reference ==

Round 15's audit had a hand-typed expected column written by the same author from the
same reading that produced the parser, so it agreed with its own bug and reported
"C matches the derived GFM value on all 27 cases". This audit replaces that column with
a separately written reference (quoted in full below) that transliterates published
GFM 0.29 sentences one at a time, in the sentences' own order, and imports nothing from
the parser. The sentences are printed above the table so each rule can be checked against
the published text without reading inventory-oracle.ts.

$ node --experimental-strip-types audit-classes.mts

== GFM 0.29 sentences this audit's reference implements ==

  BLANK          §2.1: A line containing no characters, or a line containing only spaces (U+0020) or tabs (U+0009), is called a blank line.
  WS6            §2.1: A whitespace character is a space (U+0020), tab (U+0009), newline (U+000A), line tabulation (U+000B), form feed (U+000C), or carriage return (U+000D).
  TABS           §2.2: Tabs in lines are not expanded to spaces. However, in contexts where spaces help to define block structure, tabs behave as if they were replaced by spaces with a tab stop of 4 characters.
  ATX_OPEN       §4.2: An ATX heading consists of a string of characters, parsed as inline content, between an opening sequence of 1-6 unescaped # characters and an optional closing sequence of any number of unescaped # characters.
  ATX_SEP        §4.2: The opening sequence of # characters must be followed by a space or by the end of line.
  ATX_CLOSE      §4.2: The optional closing sequence of #s must be preceded by a space and may be followed by spaces only.
  ATX_INDENT     §4.2: The opening # character may be indented 0-3 spaces.
  ATX_STRIP      §4.2: The raw contents of the heading are stripped of leading and trailing spaces before being parsed as inline content.
  ATX_LEVEL      §4.2: The heading level is equal to the number of # characters in the opening sequence.
  TABLE_PIPES    tables: The table's rows consist of cells delimited by pipes (|); the leading and trailing pipe characters are optional.
  TABLE_ESCAPE   tables: Include a pipe in a cell's content by escaping it, including inside other inline spans.
  TABLE_TRIM     tables: every published example surrounds cell content with spaces and renders it without them; the extension defines no trim over the §2.1 whitespace set.

